What the Lean Matrix is (practically)

A forced trade-off tool.

You are not “ranking everything”.
You are answering two questions per item:
	1.	How much value does this unlock right now?
	2.	How hard or risky is this to do?

That’s it.

⸻

Step-by-step: how to do it live as a team

Step 0 — Prep (2–3 minutes)

Keith does this silently:
	•	Open Miro
	•	Copy the Lean Matrix frame (2×2)
	•	One sticky note per user story
	•	Sticky format:
	•	Do not place anything yet

⸻

Step 1 — Agree on what “Value” and “Complexity” mean (2 minutes)

This is where teams usually mess up. Lock definitions first.

Say this out loud and agree:

Value =
	•	Does this unblock other work?
	•	Does this prevent rework?
	•	Does the apartment become more “usable” after this?

Complexity =
	•	Effort (story points)
	•	Risk (mistakes, safety, compliance)
	•	Dependencies (does it require other work first?)

⚠️ Complexity is NOT just time.

⸻

Step 2 — Place the obvious anchors first (5 minutes)

Do not start with debating everything.

Start with the extremes.

Ask the group:
	•	“What is clearly high value, low complexity?”
	•	“What is clearly low value, low complexity?”

Physically drag those stickies first.

Example:
	•	Remove Old Furniture → High value / Low complexity
	•	Deep Clean → Low value / Low complexity

Once anchors are down, everything else becomes easier.

⸻

Step 3 — Place one story at a time (10 minutes)

For each remaining story, ask only these two questions:
	1.	Compared to what’s already placed, is this more or less valuable?
	2.	Compared to what’s already placed, is this more or less complex?

Then:
	•	Move the sticky
	•	Stop talking

If someone disagrees:
	•	They must justify in one sentence
	•	No solution design
	•	Majority wins

⚠️ If discussion exceeds 90 seconds → park it and move on.

⸻

Step 4 — Sanity-check the matrix (5 minutes)

Now step back and ask:
	•	“Does anything feel obviously wrong?”
	•	“Is anything high effort but weirdly high priority?”
	•	“Are we mixing up ‘important’ with ‘urgent’?”

Typical corrections:
	•	Electrical work moves right (risk ↑)
	•	Painting moves up (value ↑ but only after prep)
	•	Curtains stay low value regardless of how easy they are

Make small adjustments only.

⸻

Step 5 — Identify dependencies verbally (3 minutes)

Do NOT redraw the matrix.

Just list them:
	•	“X blocks Y”
	•	“Y must happen before Z”

Write dependencies in README, not Miro.

⸻

Step 6 — Answer the only question that matters (2 minutes)

Ask:

“If we could only start two stories tomorrow, which would they be?”

Those should come from:
	•	High value / low complexity, or
	•	The earliest blocker in high value / high complexity

If they don’t → your matrix is wrong.

⸻

How to do it alone (exam / interview)

If solo:
	1.	List stories
	2.	Circle the ones that unlock others
	3.	Cross out anything cosmetic
	4.	Ask:
	•	“What breaks everything if I delay it?”
	•	“What causes rework if done late?”
	5.	Plot mentally

You don’t need a board. You need discipline.

⸻

Common mistakes (avoid these)

❌ Ranking by effort alone
❌ Putting everything in “high value”
❌ Treating Lean Matrix as a final plan
❌ Over-discussing edge cases
❌ Ignoring dependencies

⸻

One-line explanation (use this verbatim if needed)

“We used the Lean Matrix to prioritise tasks based on how much they unblock the system versus how complex and risky they are, not just how fast they are to complete.”
